\begin{problem}[Minor: Theorem 2.5(ii)'s density formula must exclude time zero]
On p.~1833, the theorem sets \(\xi_t(dx)=u(x,t)\,dx\) and asserts continuity on \(\R_+=[0,\infty)\), although arbitrary probability initial measures are allowed.
\end{problem}
Choose \(b=0,c=1,\kappa=0,\alpha=0,J=0,\xi_0=\delta_0\). These are admissible data, with mass identically one, and can be approximated by particles initially at zero and no injections or removals on each fixed finite horizon. At positive times the density is the heat kernel. At time zero there is no Lebesgue density of \(\delta_0\), whatever version of \(u\) is chosen. In particular, an absolutely continuous measure assigns zero to \(\{0\}\), whereas \(\delta_0\{0\}=1\).

\emph{Counterevidence and repair.}
The measure-valued path is nevertheless weakly continuous at zero. The proof of Lemma 4.3 on p.~1851 already makes the correct distinction:
\(\xi_t=u(\cdot,t)\,dx\) for \(t>0\), and \(\xi_0\) is specified separately. Incorporating that convention in Theorem 2.5(ii) repairs the endpoint wording. This is not a counterexample to hydrodynamic convergence.

\section{The particle construction and martingale identities}

\begin{problem}[Major: the stated coupling does not establish the required pathwise domination]
The no-removal comparison on p.~1847 is said to use exactly the same stochastic primitives, changing only the removal times to infinity. With the chronological family labels prescribed on p.~1831, this does not imply \(\xi_t^N\leq\zeta_t^N\) as measures.
\end{problem}
\begin{proof}[A finite genealogy showing the obstruction]
Consider one root, with Brownian motion, positive branching rate and no injections before the comparison time. There is a positive-probability event on which the root first has child \(1\), that child is to its left at a deterministic removal time and is removed, and no other particle is removed before the comparison time. In the no-removal system, child \(1\) survives and has a child before the root's next birth. That extra child receives chronological label \(2\).

At the root's next birth, the original system gives the newborn label \(2\), whereas the no-removal system gives the corresponding newborn label \(3\). The first uses \(B^2\), while its genealogically corresponding counterpart uses \(B^3\). Moreover, the no-removal particle already labelled \(2\) started earlier at a different space-time location. At a later time before further events, with positive probability the original particle \(2\) occupies a point occupied by none of the finitely many no-removal particles. Atomic measure domination therefore fails. The required event is obtained by prescribing finitely many disjoint Poisson event windows and open Brownian path constraints. It can also be embedded in an \(N\)-particle configuration.
\end{proof}
\emph{Counterevidence and repair.}
A valid domination coupling does exist: construct a common unremoved genealogical forest, with drivers indexed by root, parent and child number, then prune the selected system without reassigning drivers on surviving vertices. Chronological numbers can subsequently be used as bookkeeping. Nonanticipation in (S.7) and independent fresh drivers preserve the required marginal law. This explicit coupling, rather than literal reuse of the chronological array, supports the domination used in Lemmas 4.2--4.3 and the many-to-one estimates. The finding concerns the stated construction, not a failure of stochastic domination under all couplings. The invalid comparison is used directly in the compact-containment estimate (4.8) and in the passage from (4.18) to absolute continuity of every limit of the selected system. Those are core steps of Lemmas 4.2--4.3 and Theorem 2.5. A replacement coupling with the correct marginal laws is therefore needed to complete that derivation. This core proof defect is Major even though the genealogical repair supports the intended result.
